gnizing the problem we have at  the detention center  and as they get out what they call a  dismissal program  um trying to get them assistance as they  go forward you can't just give up you  have to keep trying  every time you contact someone it's an  opportunity  to get them off the street so we ask our  officers  to contact as many homeless as we can to  make sure that  maybe that's the day where they're going  to say yes to getting services  so the the outreach the referrals to the  services we're going to keep trying  we're not going to quit we're going to  do it better in 2021  well always an important piece and ever  since i've been your sheriff  my priorities have been violent crime  and last year we included  a quality of life and i think we're  going to continue in that vein  you know what i don't think we are going  to continue in that vein a lot of people  don't understand  30 percent of our tourism volume is  repeat customers  and if there's a perception in las vegas  that las vegas is not safe  we will not succeed as a police  department and what i mean succeed  is dealing with violent crime associated  with the depressed economy  and the ability to keep the workforce  robust and at the numbers that we need  to do  to be successful i'm ray spencer i'm the  lieutenant of the homicide section and  i'm here to talk about violent crime and  homicides  [Music]  2020 was a very busy year as an  investigator  it's a difficult year because what other  year has it been acceptable to walk  around with a mask on  there's no question from the sheriff to  all the leadership  on this department that we have geared  our focus towards reducing violent crime  we're the gold standard nationally when  you look at solvability rate it's at 94  and when you look at the national  average that's 60 percent  there's no other major city law  enforcement anywhere in the united  states that's close  to what we've done and i think that that  is a true testament to the  24 detectiv