them safe we  sent them home  but the day they came back i was pretty  happy to see them all here in the  building  as we move into the next phase of this  pandemic which is the vaccine is  available  i urge you all to get the vaccine please  in order to protect your families to  protect yourself  we do strongly strongly encourage you to  get the vaccine  and and i also strongly encourage you to  take care of one another  find the bright spots where you can it's  not always easy  and and try to have fun where you can i  know that it's been a long year  and i just want to thank every one of  the folks that work here i think we came  through this  stronger than we've ever been  so the other piece i want to talk about  is the budget because it's very  important  for people to understand our current  situation and what we anticipate is  going to happen into the future  um it's no secret we're going through  some budget  constraints some budget uh hard times  and people see that in society people  are out of work businesses are closed  associated with the covid and an  understanding of the budget  you know 30 percent of it comes through  the consolidated tax through the city  and the county  uh 30 percent through property tax and  30  say through sales tax and 10 percent of  self-generating  we receive a finite amount of money from  property taxes unless property taxes  increase that finite money is finite  uh sales tax has a big swing and that's  where we're affected the most  and that's where we hired the majority  of our police officers  on the police department through the  sales tax so and that sales tax is not  coming in because tourism aren't here  so what's on the back of my employees  minds am i going to get laid off  am i going to have a job tomorrow that's  the last thing i want you to worry about  especially when you're approaching a car  in the middle of the night and don't  know who's in the car i want you paying  attention who's in that car versus  whether you're going to have a job  tomorrow hopefully i don't eat my words  on this but i don't anticipate any pay  cuts  and i don't anticipate any additional  layoffs  we're relying greatly on the federal  stimulus  that we anticipate is going to come on  board but the tax numbers  are actually better than we predicted at  this point  and so i believe on the budget  issues and what we deal with on a daily  basis and what you have to be concerned  with  as an employee within the organization  there is some sunshine  associated with that my name is rich  hogan i'm the chief financial officer  here at  lbmpd i'm responsible for the  enforcement side of metro's budget  there the county is responsible for the  detention side of metro's budget  when the covent mitigation efforts  went into place the projections we had  at the time  were dire were very very very dire  we made a very conscious decision to  plan for every contingency but react to  what we actually knew  one of the strategies we had going into  it also was that our emphasis was going  to be  on preserving police officer and  corrections officer positions  and why is that we've gone to the voters  uh for  property tax initiatives uh sales tax  initiatives all of which were  were putting an additional burden on the  on the taxpayer  so to that end um as uh there were a few  functions that we eliminated  as positions became vacant as other  positions became vacant on the civilian  side we didn't fill them  as various rank and detective positions  which cost us a little bit more than a  standard  police officer position we became vacant  many of those were not  filled instead they were the positions  were put back to  patrol the combination of all those  along with our overtime reductions  um i i think has positioned us  pretty well going into next fiscal year  uh so the initial fear that we had  which was that uh in in fiscal year 20  in our  upcoming fiscal year that we would be  out of  essentially our savings and more cops or  fund balance  and out of our savings and crime  prevention act would have created the  perfect storm  but it looks like we've we've you know  dodged the worst of the  of the bullet if you will the challenges  we have on the detention side are a  little bit more significant we don't  have any  dedicated funding to the detention side  it's it's just strictly the county  contribution  uh we've cut where we could um but it is  a  uh it's going to be a a little bit more  of a difficult  uh challenge for us in next fiscal year  we don't anticipate  further cuts we don't anticipate layoffs  we don't anticipate requesting  cuts to cuts to salaries we are where we  need to be  assuming of course that nothing else  happens that we can't foresee  i've been amazed at how sensitive  most everybody is to our to our plight  and have really  taken it to heart and trying to help in  any way that they can  and all of it matters we are going to  get through this actually i am  optimistic  actually and and really um the vaccine  is what has what has created that that  optimism  and there's there's light at the end of  the tunnel that we  that we're likely to experience by a  return to some semblance of normality  from the vaccine's effects  so let's talk about police reform uh on  the hills of george floyd  but you know what i don't want to say on  the hills of george floyd let's let's  move back a couple years  and when we got audited by the  department of justice  in reference to our ois we saw the  writing on the wall we said hey we have  to make some  adjustments de-escalation was the key  component of our training  associated with that and all the  abilities to communicate verbal judo  everything that goes along with those  dynamic situations  and dealing with the people with mental  distress and the homeless  and everything else just made their way  into the police environment  we made adjustments and i'm very proud  of that and we publicized  all those things that go along with use  of force and dynamic use of force  we brought forward way before  george floyd move forward george floyd  all the  the eyes on law enforcement associated  with that and the need for reform across  the board  perceived need for reform across the  board  associated with that event okay  i embraced it i said what the officers  did associated with george floyd  was improper it was unprofessional and  it  didn't need to happen  but we have to be aware it did happen  and we have to be aware what society  perceives of law enforcement today  so i took it upon myself to say i got  your back i'm going to protect you  the local politicians here in las vegas  i'm very proud of the county commission  and the city council saying  yeah we're going to support you and a  direct result of that  is we had a decrease in crime  as compared to the other jurisdictions  that you're seeing the politicians are  pining on  the systemic racism and the  unprofessional  and the out of control police  departments  the police officers says no if you're  not going to support us we're not going  to support you  and i'm very proud of our police  department and the fact they say  this is what i signed up for this is  what i raised my right hand to do  and i'm going to do it as long as the  administration  supports me in my opinion i believe  my staff and myself is in full support  of what we do on a daily basis  and the proof is in the pudding we've  reduced crime  associated with 2020 where every other  jurisdiction in the united states didn't  and i'm very proud of that hello i'm  captain jim larochelle  from the office of the sheriff i'm here  today to talk to you about police reform  policy and the outcomes from the riots  that occurred this summer  the protests were something that we had  never seen before initially  we tried to catch up to the amount of  people that were protesting  we were surprised at the amount of  angst that was directed towards our  officers out in the street  our agency had done a number of things  to promote  reform throughout our history and we had  done a number of good things that were  recognized as best police practices  and here we were during the midst of the  protest that we were painted in the same  broad brush strokes as many agencies  across the country  so we had to go back and look at our  plans on how they're related to those  previous incidents and adjust them in  these protests  we were taking uh not only the initial  outcry but that  transformed as the night went on into  being on the receiving end of bad things  or rocks and bottles things thrown at us  that turned into skirmishes on those  protest lines between the protesters and  the police  really turning into a riot situation we  had to respond to those  i think our agency did a tremendous job  we showed restraint  and that said we did have officers hurt  and that was part of what we wanted to  go back and fix too is be able to  ensure that we're responding in a way  that's keeping our employees safe also  but also keeping in the mind the  community and also protecting the rights  of the people that were there  to make their voice known in a way  that's peaceful  and adheres to the constitution  so police reform is really about trying  to meet public's  expectations and you know certainly uh  anybody that's been in policing for a  generation has seen the public's  expectations change over time  the thought today is that i'm better  today than i was yesterday and i want to  be better tomorrow and the same thing is  with policing when we talk about reform  and certainly  today we use less use of force than we  did a generation ago  and that's a good thing and the idea is  not to eliminate  use of force in all circumstance the  idea is to  make good decisions on when and how you  use force to make sure that when you use  force it's necessary  we're not talking about handcuffing the  officers out in the street to do their  job  many times we're talking about here's a  way to do the job better here's a way  to move through your career and to  follow these guidelines  these expectations where you can have a  healthy career not impacted by something  negatively  we have initiated some of the very best  reforms  internally by ourselves from the start  when we talk about duty intervene  and sanctity life and pursuit policy  those are things  that lvmpd was a premier agency one of  the very first agencies  to incorporate and you're seeing other  agencies  do the same thing i do think that this  year that police reform will again be a  topic at the state legislative  session but having said that as i think  that lvpd is in good position  that we have set a high standard that we  have the best practices  we have the best policies and the best  training i am not nervous about  uh what will be enacted at the  legislative and how much it will affect  the way that we do business  you know without a doubt 100 certain  that our officers are  some of the best trained officers in the  country we have some of the best sound  policies  in the country that are well thought out  that provide clear direction and good  guidance  and likewise is that we do hold  ourselves accountable we do have good  oversight of our  our agency and so uh for us i think it's  a business as usual but having said that  we'll continue to  look around and incorporate new ideas  and that goes back again too is that  we're always looking to change to get  better  to be better than yesterday and to  improve upon what we're doing so don't  we don't get into that situation where  others are forcing us  to make the change so let's talk about  mental health and homelessness  it's unfortunately it's a key issue it's  a  quite often a response required  associated with those  two particular societal ills when a  community member is dealing with a  person in middle distress  or a homeless person camped out in front  of their place of business or residence  or  or wherever they may frequent on a daily  day daily basis  the answer to them is dial 9-1-1 so it's  not something that's going to go away  and it's not something that we can  ignore  so it's important for people to realize  that and that we  we responded to it in the appropriate  manner  and we realized that going forward as an  administration  and as an organization and develop tools  or give  the police officers in the response to  these particular issue tools to address  it  i'm deputy chief john mcgrath of the  tourist safety division  i'm here to talk about homelessness and  mental health going forward into 2021.  we started the homeless outreach team in  2018  and since we started that team we've  contacted over 20  000 people the homeless and the mentally  ill need help  and we are in the best position to be  the first  people to contact them and to try to get  them help  starting in 2020 we started to form  the behavioral health unit as part of  that behavioral health unit going  forward we have six  interns from unlv they're graduate  students in social work  and each of them are case managers for  some of the  what we call clients of that are that  we're trying to get more services to  and as we go in 2021 we really expect  that unit to to have more and more  results  we moved uh crisis intervention team out  of training and put them in  community engagement and so that section  now includes  crisis intervention the homeless  outreach team  and lima lima stands for law enforcement  mental health addiction we want to let  this las vegas community know  that we care about the issue of  homelessness  but we also understand that people that  are mentally ill  need help whether it's from us or us  referring them to a service or while  they're at jail  before they get out of jail at ccdc  whether we can get them  into a program where they'll be  getting assistance and not getting  arrested for  crimes that they commit and the jail  does a awesome job  of recognizing the problem we have at  the detention center  and as they get out what they call a  dismissal program  um trying to get them assistance as they  go forward you can't just give up you  have to keep trying  every time you contact someone it's an  opportunity  to get them off the street so we ask our  officers  to contact as many homeless as we can to  make sure that  maybe that's the day where they're going  to say yes to getting services  so the the outreach the referrals to the  services we're going to keep trying  we're not going to quit we're going to  do it better in 2021  well always an important piece and ever  since i've been your sheriff  my priorities have been violent crime  and last year we included  a quality of life and i think we're  going to continue in that vein  you know what i don't think we are going  to continue in that vein a lot of people  don't understand  30 percent of our tourism volume is  repeat customers  and if there's a perception in las vegas  that las vegas is not safe  we will not succeed as a police  department and what i mean succeed  is dealing with violent crime associated  with the depressed economy  and the ability to keep the workforce  robust and at the numbers that we need  to do  to be successful i'm ray spencer i'm the  lieutenant of the homicide section and  i'm here to talk about violent crime and  homicides  [Music]  2020 was a very busy year as an  investigator  it's a difficult year because what other  year has it been acceptable to walk  around with a mask on  there's no question from the sheriff to  all the leadership  on this department that we have geared  our focus towards reducing violent crime  we're the gold standard nationally when  you look at solvability rate it's at 94  and when you look at the national  average that's 60 percent  there's no other major city law  enforcement anywhere in the united  states that's close  to what we've done and i think that that  is a true testament to the  24 detectives the two investigative  specialists  and the four sergeants that work in  homicide when you look at homicide  specifically  but most importantly you know we can't  get away from the fact that the officers  that are out on the street  have done a tremendous job because we  would not be  anywhere near the success rate that we  have right now  if it wasn't for the patrol officers  that are out making those stops  and being proactive and doing the great  work that they're doing  if the community doesn't trust the  police department they're not going to  provide the tips  that we receive and because the trust  that we've built  we're getting a lot of those tips that i  don't think a lot of agencies are  getting  also our solvability percentage is so  high that the community has confidence  that when they report a crime and they  cooperate with the police  that we're going to solve that crime and  that's not just homicide that's robbery  that's burglary that's every crime that  we investigate on this agency  as an agency and as a community we need  to be very proud of that  our assistance that we're getting from  our digital forensics lab  out with the advancements in technology  are helping us solve  identify people quicker solve crimes  quicker  things that would not have been solved  10 years ago are getting solved  routinely  in 2020 and going into 2021.  the importance of making an impact on  shooting crimes is paramount and when  you look at  every 415a that we investigate  could potentially be a homicide that  we're investigating and when you look at  the impact that homicides and violent  crime have  everything that we can do to reduce  those numbers we need to make sure that  we're doing  that is where the work that the patrol  officers are doing on the street  i can tell you just from looking at some  of the involvement and cooperation  and hard work that they are doing that's  who we need to be looking at is the  success of this agency  all right so we made it through 2020  so you know the proverbial middle finger  we're gonna give that to uh  2020. and you know what let's look for  the light at the end of the tunnel folks  um we had a very tough trying year in  2020 i anticipate 2021  is going to be more positive you know  with the  onset of the vaccine people coming back  to las vegas to bolster the economy  and you know all the things that goes  along with the robust economy and a  quality of life community and in society  having a better perspective on their  daily  lives both with our family members with  internally  in our family and the community that we  deal with on a daily basis  and that we are required to serve and  that we're proud to serve  i want everybody to hold their chin up  high be proud of how we responded to  2020  and look forward to 2021 being a very  successful  positive forward moving year thank you  everyone
 

